		<item type="traktor.render.Scalar" version="traktor.render.Node:1">
			<id>{3F0A21D7-5C64-4B18-9E52-6D1A70C4B81E}</id>
			<comment><![CDATA[

Focus distance in metres; where the lens is sharp. Fixed rather than pulled to
whatever is under the crosshair, which would rack focus every time the player
turned and would need a frame of history to damp.

			]]></comment>
			<position>
				<first>860</first>
				<second>480</second>
			</position>
			<value>8</value>
		</item>
		<item type="traktor.render.Scalar" version="traktor.render.Node:1">
			<id>{3F0A21D7-5C64-4B18-9E52-6D1A70C4B81F}</id>
			<comment><![CDATA[

Half the depth of the sharp zone, in metres. With the focus above, everything
between 3 m and 13 m is untouched -- the range over which a zombie is worth
shooting at, and wide enough that walking about does not pull the world in and
out of focus. Widen this and the far ramp moves out with it.

			]]></comment>
			<position>
				<first>860</first>
				<second>550</second>
			</position>
			<value>4</value>
		</item>
		<item type="traktor.render.Scalar" version="traktor.render.Node:1">
			<id>{3F0A21D7-5C64-4B18-9E52-6D1A70C4B820}</id>
			<comment><![CDATA[

Near blur; the circle of confusion radius in pixels at 1080p for something at
the eye itself. The weapon sits about half a metre out and so takes four fifths
of it, which reads as a lens and not as a fault. Kept to single pixels for the
reason given in the script -- the near field cannot spread the way it should.

			]]></comment>
			<position>
				<first>860</first>
				<second>620</second>
			</position>
			<value>2</value>
		</item>
		<item type="traktor.render.Scalar" version="traktor.render.Node:1">
			<id>{3F0A21D7-5C64-4B18-9E52-6D1A70C4B821}</id>
			<comment><![CDATA[

Far blur; the same, for the distance. Full by 26 m and already half of it by 22,
where the fog starts -- the two carry the treeline off together, rather than the
blur waiting until the fog has finished and having nothing left to soften.

			]]></comment>
			<position>
				<first>860</first>
				<second>690</second>
			</position>
			<value>8</value>
		</item>
		<item type="traktor.render.Script" version="8,traktor.render.Node:1">
			<id>{3F0A21D7-5C64-4B18-9E52-6D1A70C4B822}</id>
			<comment/>
			<position>
				<first>1143</first>
				<second>307</second>
			</position>
			<name>Depth of field</name>
			<technique/>
			<domain>Undefined</domain>
			<localSize>
				<X>1</X>
				<Y>1</Y>
				<Z>1</Z>
			</localSize>
			<useRayTracing>false</useRayTracing>
			<include/>
			<inputPins>
				<item>
					<id>{5B2C7E10-4A31-4D96-8F27-91C6E3A50041}</id>
					<name>Source</name>
				</item>
				<item>
					<id>{5B2C7E10-4A31-4D96-8F27-91C6E3A50042}</id>
					<name>SourceState</name>
				</item>
				<item>
					<id>{5B2C7E10-4A31-4D96-8F27-91C6E3A50043}</id>
					<name>Depth</name>
				</item>
				<item>
					<id>{5B2C7E10-4A31-4D96-8F27-91C6E3A50044}</id>
					<name>DepthState</name>
				</item>
				<item>
					<id>{5B2C7E10-4A31-4D96-8F27-91C6E3A50045}</id>
					<name>TexCoord</name>
				</item>
				<item>
					<id>{5B2C7E10-4A31-4D96-8F27-91C6E3A50046}</id>
					<name>Center</name>
				</item>
				<item>
					<id>{5B2C7E10-4A31-4D96-8F27-91C6E3A50047}</id>
					<name>FocusDistance</name>
				</item>
				<item>
					<id>{5B2C7E10-4A31-4D96-8F27-91C6E3A50048}</id>
					<name>FocusRange</name>
				</item>
				<item>
					<id>{5B2C7E10-4A31-4D96-8F27-91C6E3A50049}</id>
					<name>NearBlur</name>
				</item>
				<item>
					<id>{5B2C7E10-4A31-4D96-8F27-91C6E3A5004A}</id>
					<name>FarBlur</name>
				</item>
			</inputPins>
			<outputPins>
				<item>
					<id>{5B2C7E10-4A31-4D96-8F27-91C6E3A50051}</id>
					<name>Output</name>
					<type>Vector</type>
				</item>
			</outputPins>
			<script><![CDATA[

// Depth of field.
//
// The projection above has already worked out where in the source image this
// pixel comes from, and the sampler feeding $Center has fetched that one texel.
// All this does is decide how *wide* that read should have been. A lens brings
// exactly one distance to a point; everything nearer or further arrives spread
// over a disc -- the circle of confusion -- so a pixel looking at something out
// of focus is a disc's worth of the image around it rather than a texel of it.
//
// Gathered here in the projection's own pass instead of in the usual
// downsample/bokeh/composite chain of its own. The scene is fogged out by 22 m
// and lit by what the player is carrying, so the far field is a low-contrast
// wash long before the disc grows wide enough for a half resolution pass to pay
// for itself, and the near field wants a radius of a few pixels, which is
// cheapest taken at full rate. Everything inside the sharp zone -- most of a
// frame -- costs one comparison and skips the loop.

const vec2 uv = $TexCoord.xy;
const vec2 size = vec2(textureSize(sampler2D($Source, $SourceState), 0));

// Radii below are pixels at 1080p and scale from there, so that resizing the
// window changes how much of the world is in shot and not how defocused it is.
const float pixelScale = size.y / 1080.0f;

// The sharp zone, and the ramps leading out of either side of it. Held as plain
// distances rather than as an aperture and a focal length: a real lens open wide
// enough to be worth simulating has a depth of field centimetres deep, which is
// the wrong answer for a game where whatever is being shot at may be anywhere
// between the end of the barrel and the treeline.
const float focus = $FocusDistance;
const float range = $FocusRange;
const float nearEdge = max(focus - range, 0.01f);
const float farEdge = focus + range;

// The far ramp reaches full width one sharp zone beyond the sharp zone, which
// for the defaults is 26 m -- just past where this scene's fog begins, so that
// the two take the distance away together instead of in turn.
const float farRamp = max(farEdge, 0.01f);

// The disc, in units of the circle of confusion radius. A golden angle spiral;
// it spreads evenly over the disc at any tap count and leaves no axis for the
// eye to catch on, which concentric rings do.
vec2 c_taps[] =
{
	vec2(0.06406f, 0.16476f),
	vec2(-0.27458f, -0.13548f),
	vec2(0.37953f, -0.11048f),
	vec2(-0.24282f, 0.39973f),
	vec2(-0.10315f, -0.52020f),
	vec2(0.47256f, 0.34704f),
	vec2(-0.63365f, 0.06883f),
	vec2(0.45195f, -0.51429f),
	vec2(0.01506f, 0.72871f),
	vec2(-0.53213f, -0.55730f),
	vec2(0.80828f, 0.05413f),
	vec2(-0.66201f, 0.52962f),
	vec2(0.13594f, -0.87337f),
	vec2(0.50893f, 0.76469f),
	vec2(-0.92424f, -0.22809f),
	vec2(0.86391f, -0.47160f)
};

// View depth in metres, straight out of the gbuffer's red channel, read at the
// projected coordinate because that is the pixel this one is showing. Point
// sampled throughout -- a filtered pair of depths across a silhouette gives a
// distance at which nothing stands.
const float z = textureLod(sampler2D($Depth, $DepthState), uv, 0.0f).x;
const float coc = pixelScale * max(
	$NearBlur * clamp((nearEdge - z) / nearEdge, 0.0f, 1.0f),
	$FarBlur * smoothstep(0.0f, 1.0f, clamp((z - farEdge) / farRamp, 0.0f, 1.0f))
);

vec3 color = $Center.rgb;

// Under a pixel of confusion a disc has nothing to add to the texel already
// fetched.
if (coc > 1.0f)
{
	// Turn the fixed kernel by a per pixel angle. Without it a bright point out
	// of focus resolves into sixteen copies of itself; with it, into noise, which
	// reads as grain rather than as a pattern.
	const vec2 px = uv * size;
	const float a = 6.2831853f * fract(52.9829189f * fract(0.06711056f * px.x + 0.00583715f * px.y));
	const vec2 rot = vec2(cos(a), sin(a));

	float weight = 1.0f;

	for (int i = 0; i < 16; ++i)
	{
		const vec2 t = c_taps[i];
		const vec2 o = vec2(t.x * rot.x - t.y * rot.y, t.x * rot.y + t.y * rot.x) * coc;
		const vec2 tuv = uv + o / size;

		const float tz = textureLod(sampler2D($Depth, $DepthState), tuv, 0.0f).x;
		const float tcoc = pixelScale * max(
			$NearBlur * clamp((nearEdge - tz) / nearEdge, 0.0f, 1.0f),
			$FarBlur * smoothstep(0.0f, 1.0f, clamp((tz - farEdge) / farRamp, 0.0f, 1.0f))
		);

		// A tap only reaches this pixel if its own disc is wide enough to cover
		// the distance between the two -- a gather standing in for the scatter
		// that physically happens. Without the test a sharp figure against a soft
		// background bleeds outwards into it, the background pixel happily
		// collecting the figure's colour although the figure, being in focus,
		// spreads nowhere. The converse case is the one this pass cannot answer:
		// a defocused foreground should spread over the sharp background behind
		// it, but a sharp pixel never enters the loop to collect it, so the near
		// field keeps a harder edge than it should. That is why $NearBlur is set
		// in single pixels while $FarBlur is not.
		const float w = clamp(tcoc - length(o) + 1.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f);

		color += textureLod(sampler2D($Source, $SourceState), tuv, 0.0f).rgb * w;
		weight += w;
	}

	color /= weight;
}

$Output = vec4(color, 1.0f);

]]></script>
		</item>
	</nodes>
